meta {
  name: process image upload v2
  type: http
  seq: 1
}
post {
  url: {{SUPABASE_URL}}/functions/v1/process-image-upload-v2
  body: multipartForm
  auth: none
}
headers {
  apikey: {{SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY}}
  Authorization: Bearer {{SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN}}
}
body:multipart-form {
  file: @file({{IMAGE_FILE_PATH}})
  permit: {{IMAGE_UPLOAD_PERMIT}}
  signature: {{IMAGE_UPLOAD_SIGNATURE}}
}
docs {
  First obtain permit and signature from the application's POST /api/images/authorise
  using the same signed-in user. Its JSON metadata includes target, bytes, mimeType,
  sha256 (lowercase SHA-256 of the exact file), optional destination and optional crop.
  Browser metadata authorisation requires the app session cookie and matching Origin.
  The Next.js server selects all dimensions, encoding, bucket and visibility settings
  from src/supacharger.adapters/media/image-targets.ts. Never place the signing secret here.

  Send exactly file, permit and signature to this Edge endpoint. Do not re-serialise
  permit or change the file; the signature and hash must match. No target, crop or
  destination multipart fields are accepted. Do not manually set multipart Content-Type.
  The permit expires after five minutes and is bound to the signed-in user. It may be
  reused with the same file before expiry; it is not single-use. The JWT remains required.

  Edge validates the permit then auto-orients, crops, resizes without upscaling, strips
  metadata and stores using user-scoped Storage RLS. No service-role storage client is used.
  JPEG/PNG/WebP inputs; JPEG/PNG outputs. Policy limits are at most 10MiB, 4096px per
  output dimension and 40 megapixels (null retains the configured pixel-cap opt-out).
  Starter profile policies remain private JPEG avatar/header images with one-hour URLs.

  200: target, bucket, path, content_type, width, height, bytes, and configured public_url
  or signed_url/signed_url_expires_in. Persist the returned path through the owning form.
  400: invalid, missing, duplicate or unknown multipart fields. 401: invalid user JWT.
  403: invalid signature, expired/wrong-user permit, altered policy or mismatched file.
  405: wrong method. 413: input byte limit. 415: unsupported format. 422: invalid image,
  crop, dimensions or pixel limit. 500: processing/Storage failure. 503: signing unconfigured.
  See docs/agents/image-transformation.md for secret configuration and staged rollout.
}
